-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('13695ebd-8121-5451-b622-b5bdb0533b5f', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Playing the Sound of a Healthy Reef$q$, $q$A healthy coral reef is a noisy place. Shrimp snap their claws, fish grunt and chatter, and waves move across the rough surface of the coral. For young fish drifting in open water after hatching, this sound appears to work like a beacon. Many species seem to follow it when they choose a reef on which to settle and grow.

When a reef is damaged by storms or by warm water that bleaches the coral, it grows quiet. Fewer animals live there, so there is less to hear, and young fish may pass it by. That silence can trap a damaged reef in a cycle: without new fish to graze on seaweed and keep the coral clean, recovery becomes even slower.

Marine biologist Nadia Ferreira and her team, working at a fictional research station off the island of Pelandra, wondered whether sound itself could help break that cycle. They recorded the noise of a thriving reef using underwater microphones. Then they placed small speakers on patches of damaged reef and played the recordings every night for six weeks. Other damaged patches nearby received silent speakers so the team could compare the results.

The patches with sound attracted about twice as many young fish as the silent patches, and the new arrivals included many different species, not just a few. Some of those species graze on seaweed, the very work a struggling reef needs. Ferreira's team cautioned, however, that sound cannot rebuild coral on its own. If the water stays too warm or polluted, fish that arrive may not survive, and the coral may continue to decline.

The researchers describe the speakers as a way to give recovery a head start rather than a cure. They suggest combining sound with coral replanting and with efforts to protect water quality. Still, the experiment changes how scientists think about restoration. A reef is not only something to see but something to hear, and restoring its voice may help bring back the life that made it noisy in the first place.$q$, 'draft')
