-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d249cb97-0a2d-5b50-85a8-e9c10880ce29', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Pens, Keyboards and the Work of Listening$q$, $q$Many students bring laptops to lectures, confident that typing lets them capture more of what they hear. A study at the fictional Brennick College tested whether that confidence is justified. Researchers asked one group of students to take notes by hand and another to type them while watching the same recorded lecture. A week later, both groups answered questions about the material.

The typists produced longer notes, often copying the speaker's sentences nearly word for word. Yet on questions that required understanding ideas rather than recalling facts, the handwriting group scored noticeably higher. The researchers proposed an explanation. Because writing by hand is slower, students cannot record everything, so they must decide what matters and put it in their own words. That extra mental work seems to help them learn.

The story did not end there. A second team, led by psychologist Ines Valborg, wondered whether the tool itself was responsible or the way students used it. In a follow-up study, Valborg gave some typists a simple instruction: summarize the lecture in your own words instead of copying it. Those students wrote shorter notes, and their scores on understanding questions rose to nearly match those of the handwriting group.

Valborg's results suggest that the important difference is not between paper and keyboard but between copying and thinking. A pen happens to force students to condense and rephrase, while a keyboard makes copying easy. Students who type can still learn well if they deliberately slow down and process ideas as they write.

These findings matter for classrooms that are moving toward digital tools. Banning laptops may not be necessary, and it could harm students who rely on typing for good reasons. Teaching students how to take notes, however, may matter more than which device they hold. Whatever the tool, the notes that help most are the ones that require the learner to think.$q$, 'draft')
