   $q$Which reading of the final two lines is best supported, given what the poem does and does not say?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The cousin admits she invented most of her thirty-one marks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstated-inference"},{"text":"The cousin corrects her count only because the speaker scolded her.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The cousin crosses out a mark to make the two counts match exactly.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The cousin may have reconsidered one mark, though the poem never says why.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The cousin may have reconsidered one mark, though the poem never says why.$q$, $q$Crossing out "one of her own" suggests the speaker's answer made her rethink at least one mark, but the poem gives no reason and she only shrugged. One crossed-out mark cannot support the claim that she invented most of her count.$q$, 'draft', '00982fa1-3251-5590-803f-299382e8b1d7')
