-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('eacf0794-443e-57e2-976c-cb98fadcf054', 'rg12-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Put the Chess Tables Back$q$, $q$For twenty-two years, the four stone chess tables at the north end of Fenley Square were rarely empty. On summer evenings, retirees played students, shift workers on their dinner break played anyone who would sit down, and a small crowd usually gathered to watch the closest game. Last spring, when the square was repaved, the tables were hauled away and replaced with a row of planters. The planters are attractive. They are also, as far as I can tell, used by no one.

The Harrowgate parks department has defended the change by arguing that the tables served only a small group of regular players. Its own numbers tell a different story. A count the department conducted in 2023, before the renovation, found an average of thirty-one people using or watching the tables each weekday evening, more than used the square's fountain, benches and lawn combined during the same hours.

Numbers alone, however, do not capture what the tables did. They were one of the few places in town where a sixteen-year-old and a seventy-year-old might spend an hour together as equals, each trying to outthink the other. Kofi Mensah, who taught many of the younger players, told me that he met most of his closest friends across those boards. Planters do not start conversations.

The department raises a reasonable concern about cost. Stone tables are heavy, and reinstalling them would require new footings. Yet the original tables are sitting in a city storage yard, undamaged, and the department's own estimate puts the reinstallation at about the price of a single season of planter maintenance.

The parks board meets on the first Tuesday of next month. Anyone who ever played, watched or simply walked past those games should attend and ask for one thing: put the tables back where they belong.$q$, 'draft')
