-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ed1236dc-c892-58b3-afb1-b7df57f817ba', 'rg12-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Chart Sailors Could Trust$q$, $q$In a glass case at the Port Ellery Maritime Museum lies a map of the Varnell Coast, drawn in 1857. Visitors usually admire its delicate lettering and the tiny sketches of headlands in its margins. Few realize that the map was accurate enough to remain in use by harbor pilots for almost seventy years, or that producing it took a team of nine people more than four summers.

The survey began in 1853, after two cargo ships ran aground within a single month on a reef that older charts placed nearly a mile from its true position. The harbor commission hired Henrik Dahlgren, an engineer who had worked on inland canal surveys, and gave him a simple instruction: produce a chart that sailors could trust.

Dahlgren's method was triangulation. His team first measured one straight baseline along a flat beach with extraordinary care, laying wooden rods end to end over three kilometers. From each end of that line they sighted a distant marker, such as a church tower or a stone pile they built on a hilltop, and measured the angles with a brass instrument. Knowing one side and two angles of a triangle, they could calculate the other sides. Each new side then became the base of another triangle, and the chain of triangles crept north along the coast.

The calculations fell to Rosa Altamirano, who had trained as a bookkeeper. Every evening she checked the day's angles, and whenever the three angles of a triangle failed to add up correctly within a small margin, she sent the team back the next morning to measure again. Her insistence on repetition slowed the work and frustrated the crew, but it is the main reason the finished chart held up so well.

By the autumn of 1856 the chain of triangles reached Cape Morrow, and the team spent the following winter drawing the final chart. The reef that had caught the cargo ships appeared on it in its correct place. No vessel ran aground there again for the rest of the century.$q$, 'draft')
