-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f6a178ff-9154-5d93-a1e8-b54af78d5e1a', 'rg12-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Listening for Leaks$q$, $q$The Problem

The city of Brennick pumps about ninety million liters of treated water into its pipes every day, and roughly a fifth of it never reaches a faucet. It seeps out through cracked joints and corroded mains buried under the streets. Most of these leaks are too small to bubble up to the surface, so crews used to find them only after a pipe finally burst.

How the Sensors Listen

In 2022 the water utility began attaching small acoustic sensors to fire hydrants, which connect directly to the mains below. Water escaping under pressure makes a faint, steady hiss that travels along the metal pipe. Each night, between two and four in the morning, when traffic is light and few people are using water, the sensors record a few minutes of sound. Software then compares recordings from neighboring hydrants. Because the hiss reaches the nearer sensor slightly sooner, the difference in arrival time lets the software estimate where along the pipe the leak lies, often within a meter or two.

Early Results

During the first year, the sensors flagged 214 suspected leaks in the two districts where they were installed. Crews dug at 190 of those sites and found a leak at 171. The utility estimates that repairing them saves about four million liters a day, and main breaks in the two districts fell by nearly half compared with the year before.

What Remains Uncertain

The system works best on metal pipes, which carry sound well. Brennick's newer plastic mains muffle the hiss, and the sensors have missed several leaks in those sections. Priya Raghunathan, the engineer who manages the program, says the utility is testing a more sensitive sensor before expanding the system to the rest of the city.$q$, 'draft')
