-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('51bc837d-fabf-5271-8c35-7d7749629811', 'rg12-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Ways to Build a Rower$q$, $q$Coaches of endurance sports have argued for decades about how athletes should spend their training hours. One camp favors long, steady sessions at a pace that still allows conversation. The other favors short, punishing intervals followed by rest. A study of the rowing program at Kessler University offers one of the clearest side-by-side comparisons yet, though its findings are less tidy than either camp might hope.

Researchers led by exercise physiologist Mateo Ferreira divided forty college rowers into two groups for twelve weeks. The steady group trained mostly at low intensity, rowing for seventy to ninety minutes at a time. The interval group trained for less total time, mixing bursts of near-maximum effort with periods of easy paddling. Both groups completed the same number of sessions each week.

On the first measure, a two-thousand-meter race on a rowing machine, the two groups improved by almost exactly the same amount. On the second, a test of how much oxygen an athlete's body can use, the interval group gained more. On the third, the number of training days lost to soreness or minor injury, the steady group came out ahead, missing about a third as many sessions.

Ferreira's team draws a cautious conclusion. Intervals appear to raise an athlete's ceiling faster, but steady work is easier on the body and lets athletes train more consistently. The largest individual improvements, the researchers note, were spread across both groups, which suggests that how each athlete responds may matter as much as the method itself.

For coaches, the practical lesson may be that the old argument poses a false choice. A season that builds a broad base of steady rowing and then adds intervals as racing approaches could capture the advantages of each. Ferreira's team plans to test exactly that schedule next year.$q$, 'draft')
