-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e13bfab3-c982-5ce4-9253-9488b16c5297', 'rg12-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Curtain Under the Soot$q$, $q$When conservator Hana Kobayashi first unrolled the painted curtain of the Alder Falls Opera House, she found a dim brown landscape: a river, a stone bridge, and a row of trees that were nearly impossible to tell apart. Records showed the curtain had been painted in 1911 in shades of green, blue and gold. The question was whether those colors were gone or merely hidden.

To find out, Kobayashi cleaned a patch the size of a postage stamp in one corner. She rolled a cotton swab dampened with a mild solution across the surface, and the swab came away nearly black. Beneath it, a bright blue sky appeared. The colors had survived. A century of soot from gas lamps, coal furnaces and wood stoves had simply settled over them.

That small test shaped the entire project. Because the paint underneath was intact, the goal changed from repainting the curtain to cleaning it, which is slower but preserves the original artist's work. Kobayashi and two assistants divided the eleven-meter curtain into a grid of numbered squares, each about the size of a sheet of paper, and cleaned them one at a time over fourteen months.

Cleaning was not always enough. In places where the canvas had cracked along old folds, flakes of paint had fallen away entirely. There the team filled the gaps with a reversible paint, matched by eye to the surrounding colors, so that a future conservator could remove the additions without harming the original.

The restored curtain was lowered for the first time last October, before a performance by the town's youth orchestra. The river is green again, and the bridge is gray stone instead of brown shadow. Visitors who look closely can still find one small patch of the old soot, which Kobayashi left on purpose in a lower corner as a record of what the curtain looked like before.$q$, 'draft')
