-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6e07af05-c302-5308-bdee-146ba797d760', 'rg12-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Clear No$q$, $q$Halima Diallo spent six weeks of her summer research program growing bread mold. Her question was simple: would an extract of crushed clove leaves slow the spread of mold on bread kept in a warm room? She had read that cloves contain compounds that fight some microbes, and she had already imagined the poster she would present at the closing symposium, with a bold headline announcing a natural preservative.

The data refused to cooperate. Her treated slices grew mold at almost exactly the same rate as her untreated ones. Two dishes on the treated side looked slightly better, and for one long evening Halima wondered whether the others might have been contaminated. If she left those out, the remaining numbers leaned, faintly, in the direction she wanted.

She brought the spreadsheet to her mentor, Dr. Esteban Morales, expecting him to help her find the flaw. He scrolled through the columns twice. "Did anything happen to the dishes you want to remove?" he asked. Halima admitted that nothing had, except that they disagreed with her. Dr. Morales nodded slowly. "Then they are not contaminated. They are inconvenient. Those are different problems."

He told her about a study of his own, years earlier, that had found nothing at all. A researcher in another country later read it and decided not to spend three years chasing the same idea. "A clear no saves someone else a lot of time," he said. "A blurry yes wastes it."

Halima rebuilt her poster over the weekend. The headline now read, in plain letters, "Clove Leaf Extract Did Not Slow Mold Growth." Beneath it she listed every dish, including the ones she had nearly erased, and a section titled "What I Would Test Next." At the symposium, fewer visitors stopped than at the poster about solar ovens across the aisle. But a graduate student who did stop asked for her data file, because she was planning a similar experiment and wanted to know where not to look.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
