   $q$A student's report on glaciers says: "Glaciers move. They move very slowly. Some move only a few inches a day." Which revision uses punctuation for the best effect?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Glaciers, move. They, move very slowly. Some, move only a few inches a day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-error"},{"text":"Glaciers move. They move very slowly. Some move only a few inches a day!","is_correct":true},{"text":"Glaciers move!!! They move very slowly!!! Some move only a few inches a day!!!","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overuses-exclamation"},{"text":"Glaciers move? They move very slowly? Some move only a few inches a day?","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-end-mark"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Glaciers move. They move very slowly. Some move only a few inches a day!$q$, $q$Saving one exclamation point for the most surprising fact makes that fact stand out. Putting exclamation points everywhere is tempting, but when every sentence shouts, none of them stands out.$q$, 'draft', null)
